.TH PACMKR-REPO 1 "2026-09-03" "pacmkr 0.1.0" "pacmkr Manual"
.SH NAME
pacmkr\-repo \- manage a local pacman package repository
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pacmkr\-repo
.I command
.RI [ arguments ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pacmkr\-repo
creates and maintains a local repository database of the kind
.BR pacman (8)
reads. It wraps
.BR repo\-add (8)
and
.BR repo\-remove (8)
with a small registry so repositories can be referred to by name.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "create " "name directory"
Register
.I name
as a repository rooted at
.IR directory .
The directory is recorded now; the database file is generated when the first
package is added.
.TP
.BI "add " "name package.pkg.tar.zst ..."
Copy one or more package archives (and any adjacent
.B .sig
signatures) into the repository and add them to its database.
.TP
.BI "remove " "name package-name ..."
Remove the named packages from the repository database.
.TP
.B list
Print each registered repository and its directory, tab\-separated.
.TP
.BI "delete " name
Unregister
.IR name .
The database and package files are deliberately left in place.
.TP
.BR help ", " \-\-help ", " \-h
Show usage.
.SH NOTES
Adding a repository to a running system still requires a
.B [name]
section in
.BR pacman.conf (5)
pointing at the generated database with
.RB \(dq Server\ =\ file://... \(dq.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns
.B 0
on success and non\-zero on error (unknown command, missing arguments, or a
failing
.BR repo\-add (8)
invocation).
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
pacmkr\-repo create myrepo ~/.local/share/pacmkr/myrepo
pacmkr\-repo add myrepo ./hello\-1.0\-1\-x86_64.pkg.tar.zst
pacmkr\-repo remove myrepo hello
pacmkr\-repo list
pacmkr\-repo delete myrepo
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pacmkr (1),
.BR repo\-add (8),
.BR pacman.conf (5)
